x86_64-linux  Every difference is a remainder recorded before this case, and none is the primary base's: the names the layout produces - `_ZTV1X`, `_ZN1XC1Ev`, and the C2 constructors of A, V and D1 that it calls - agree with clang, and the vtable's words are compared in the header of the .cpp. (1) The **VTT and the construction vtable**, `_ZTT1X` and `_ZTC1X0_2D1`, which clang emits so that a virtual call from inside D1's constructor, while it is X's base, reaches the right level; cxx1 emits neither and refuses that shape - virtual-base-diamond.nonames sets it out. With them goes `_ZTV2D1`, which cxx1 emits and clang does not: clang builds D1 only ever as X's base, through the construction vtable, so D1's own table is never wanted here. (2) **`_ZTI1X` and `_ZTS1X`**: X has two bases, which is the `__vmi_class_type_info` shape this compiler does not write, so it leaves the vtable's type_info word zero; virtual-base-vbtable.nonames records the same for R. `_ZTI1A` and `_ZTS1A` go with them - clang names A's type_info only from inside X's, which cxx1 has not got. (3) cxx1 emits `_ZN1AC1Ev`, `_ZN1VC1Ev`, `_ZN2D1C1Ev` and `_ZN1XC2Ev`, the complete-object constructors of the three bases and the base-subobject constructor of X, none of which this file calls - nothing builds an A, a V or a D1 on its own, and nothing derives from X. An unused definition is not a wrong one; cxx1 emits C1 beside every C2, the way virtual-base-diamond.nonames records for the destructors.
arm64-darwin  The same three, with Mach-O's leading underscore.
